text:
They took the guest’s armor away, and invited him inside;  
the woman followed behind, and Jokhola goes ahead;  
Zviadauri follows along too, as a sworn brother has been found.

The sun had not yet risen, the dew still slept on the grass,  
the breeze had not yet blown, had not yet spread below,  
countless men and women had gathered on the meadow.  
Zviadauri was being brought along, hands bound, amid the crowd;  
everyone rejoiced at his killing—who then would begin the mourning?!  
Death frightens us all; if it is another’s they are killing, we want to watch;  
men often do not feel how great a sin they are committing.  
How many evil souls I know who walk with untroubled brows;  
and who would not wish to crush the one who torments them?!

XII. The army of the Khevsurs comes down, with the banner borne before it;  
they made for the cemetery, the silent dwelling of the Kists:  
let them seek their dead, gather the hero’s bones,  
and beneath the tormentors of Zviadauri dig out their eyes.

They were met by the Kists’ army too, hidden at the edge of the ravine;  
they fired their guns, with bandoliers strapped on;  
the Khევსur boys, leaping up, do not yield the road.  
From both sides they shoot, shouting over their guns;  
both sides are troubled by the matter, both fight steadfastly.  
The bullet drank plenty of blood — it fed on the gorge — yet the Kists did not break,  
they stand like a fortress tower.  
Baburauli advised the Khევსurs to go down sword to sword.  
The matter came to swords, and the shield gave way for cutting a path;  
the Khევსurs have a costly treasure and spoils to defend.  
Pharo, do not lie to Gordas; you are his lackey;  
in front of the Khევსurs running downhill, a cliffy precipice appeared.

The Kist host fled, and with a roar it made for the towers;  
the Khevsurs pursued behind, with the gleam of helmets on their heads;  
on the cemetery they gathered Zviada’s bones in a whirl:  
some lay here, some there, torn by birds, fluttering in the wind.

The wind blows, below it whistles through the valleys from the mountains;  
a woman stands, gazing at the river, on the cliff, with her hair spread loose;  
it reminds me of a dimmed star with pale, trembling jaws.  

She does not utter a sound… startled, she trembles as she looks at the river;  
how dreadfully it roars, how dreadfully it rushes on!  
The black gorge, bared of its teeth, fierce and savage, rages,  
“Do not kill yourself!” if only someone would at least advise her.  
The woman closed her eyes and swiftly plunged into the whirlpool.
